D={
"Victor Hugo":"Victor Hugo (1802–1885) was a leading figure of French Romanticism, writing poetry, plays and novels. The Hunchback of Notre-Dame (1831) helped save the cathedral by reviving interest in Gothic architecture. A supporter of the 1848 Republic, he denounced Louis-Napoléon's coup in 1851 and spent 19 years in exile on Jersey and Guernsey, where he finished Les Misérables (1862), a story of the ex-convict Jean Valjean, poverty and the June Rebellion of 1832. He campaigned against the death penalty and for universal education. Two million people attended his funeral in Paris.",
"Charles Dickens":"Charles Dickens (1812–1870) was sent at 12 to work in a boot-blacking factory when his father was imprisoned for debt, an experience that shaped his concern for the poor. He became a journalist and then the most popular novelist of Victorian Britain, publishing in monthly instalments: The Pickwick Papers, Oliver Twist (workhouses and child crime), A Christmas Carol (1843), David Copperfield, Bleak House, Hard Times (industrial towns), A Tale of Two Cities (the French Revolution) and Great Expectations. His vivid characters such as Scrooge and Fagin, and his criticism of social injustice, influenced reforms. He gave reading tours in Britain and America.",
"Jane Austen":"Jane Austen (1775–1817), a clergyman's daughter in Hampshire, wrote six novels — Sense and Sensibility (1811), Pride and Prejudice (1813), Mansfield Park, Emma, Northanger Abbey and Persuasion — published anonymously ('By a Lady'). With irony and wit she portrayed the gentry, marriage, money and women's limited options in Georgian England, set against the backdrop of the Napoleonic Wars. She never married and died at 41. Her novels pioneered free indirect speech and remain hugely popular through films and adaptations.",
"Mark Twain":"Samuel Clemens (1835–1910) grew up in Hannibal, Missouri, on the Mississippi, where he became a riverboat pilot; his pen name means 'two fathoms deep'. As a journalist and humorist he wrote The Adventures of Tom Sawyer (1876) and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn (1884), in which the boy Huck helps Jim, an escaped slave, a novel Hemingway said all modern American literature comes from. He criticised racism, imperialism and hypocrisy. In 1896 he toured India and wrote in Following the Equator that India was 'the cradle of the human race'.",
"Lu Xun":"Lu Xun (pen name of Zhou Shuren, 1881–1936) studied medicine in Japan but turned to literature after seeing a slide of Chinese spectators watching passively as a countryman was executed. His 'A Madman's Diary' (1918), the first major story in vernacular Chinese, attacked Confucian tradition as 'cannibalism'. 'The True Story of Ah Q' (1921) satirised Chinese apathy after the 1911 revolution. He is considered the father of modern Chinese literature and was later championed by Mao.",
"Murasaki Shikibu":"Murasaki Shikibu (about 973–1014) was a lady-in-waiting to Empress Shōshi at the Heian court in Kyoto. Educated in Chinese classics by eavesdropping on her brother's lessons, she wrote The Tale of Genji, about the loves and life of the 'Shining Prince' Genji, in 54 chapters using Japanese kana script. It portrays court life, poetry and the Buddhist sense of impermanence (mono no aware). Often called the world's first novel, it has been illustrated in scrolls since the 12th century. She also kept a diary of court life.",
"Chinua Achebe":"Chinua Achebe (1930–2013), an Igbo writer from Nigeria, wrote Things Fall Apart (1958), the story of Okonkwo, a proud leader whose world collapses under British colonialism and Christian missionaries. Written partly in response to European portrayals of Africa, such as Conrad's Heart of Darkness, which he criticised as racist, it has sold over 20 million copies in 50 languages. He wrote Arrow of God and No Longer at Ease, supported Biafra in the Nigerian civil war, and taught in the US after a car accident left him paralysed.",
"Gabriel García Márquez":"Gabriel García Márquez (1927–2014), known as 'Gabo', was a Colombian journalist and novelist raised by his grandparents in Aracataca, whose stories inspired his fiction. One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967), the saga of the Buendía family in the fictional town of Macondo, made 'magical realism' famous and sold tens of millions of copies. He also wrote Love in the Time of Cholera and Chronicle of a Death Foretold, and received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982. His work reflects Latin America's history of colonialism, civil wars and dictatorship.",
"Vincent van Gogh":"Vincent van Gogh (1853–1890), a Dutch pastor's son, worked as an art dealer and lay preacher among coal miners before becoming a painter at 27. Influenced by Impressionism and Japanese prints in Paris, he moved to Arles in southern France in 1888 and painted brilliant, emotional works with thick brushstrokes: Sunflowers, The Bedroom, Café Terrace at Night. After a breakdown in which he cut off part of his ear, he entered an asylum at Saint-Rémy, where he painted The Starry Night (1889). Supported financially by his brother Theo, he sold almost nothing in his lifetime and died of a gunshot wound at 37.",
"Pablo Picasso":"Pablo Picasso (1881–1973), born in Málaga, Spain, was a child prodigy who spent most of his life in France. His Blue Period and Rose Period gave way to Les Demoiselles d'Avignon (1907), inspired partly by African masks, and with Georges Braque he founded Cubism, showing objects from many viewpoints at once. In 1937 he painted Guernica in response to the bombing of the Basque town by Nazi and Italian planes during the Spanish Civil War; it became the century's most famous anti-war image. He produced about 50,000 works in painting, sculpture, ceramics and prints.",
"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart":"Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart (1756–1791) was born in Salzburg and toured Europe's courts as a child prodigy with his father Leopold and sister Nannerl, composing from age five. Moving to Vienna, he wrote over 600 works, including 41 symphonies, 27 piano concertos and operas such as The Marriage of Figaro (1786), Don Giovanni (1787) and The Magic Flute (1791). Despite success he was often in debt. He died at 35 while composing his Requiem, leaving it unfinished; rumours that rival Salieri poisoned him are unfounded.",
"Ludwig van Beethoven":"Ludwig van Beethoven (1770–1827) was born in Bonn and moved to Vienna, where he studied briefly with Haydn. From his late twenties he began to lose his hearing, writing in the 1802 Heiligenstadt Testament of his despair, yet he composed his greatest works when almost completely deaf. His nine symphonies, including the Third ('Eroica'), originally dedicated to Napoleon until he crowned himself emperor, the Fifth, and the Ninth with its 'Ode to Joy' (now the European anthem), bridged Classical and Romantic music. His piano sonatas and late string quartets remain landmarks.",
"Tansen":"Tansen (about 1500–1586), born Ramtanu near Gwalior, trained with Swami Haridas of Vrindavan and in the dhrupad tradition of Raja Man Singh Tomar's Gwalior court. He served the Rewa court before Akbar summoned him in 1562; he became one of the nine jewels (navaratnas) of Akbar's court. He composed ragas such as Miyan ki Malhar, Miyan ki Todi and Darbari Kanada, and legends say his singing of raga Deepak lit lamps and Megh Malhar brought rain. His tomb in Gwalior hosts the Tansen Samaroh music festival every December.",
"Raja Ravi Varma":"Raja Ravi Varma (1848–1906), from the royal family of Kilimanoor in Travancore, learned oil painting from a visiting Dutch-trained artist. He painted portraits of royalty and scenes from the Mahabharata, Ramayana and Kalidasa's works, such as Shakuntala and Hamsa Damayanti, combining European realism with Indian subjects and sarees. In 1894 he set up a lithographic press near Mumbai, producing cheap colour prints of gods and goddesses that reached ordinary homes and shaped how Indians imagine their deities. Some critics accused him of Westernising Indian art.",
"Dadasaheb Phalke":"Dhundiraj Govind Phalke (1870–1944) was a printer, photographer and magician in Maharashtra. Inspired by watching The Life of Christ in a Bombay cinema in 1911, he pawned his wife's jewellery to learn filmmaking in London. He made India's first full-length feature film, Raja Harishchandra (1913), a mythological drama in which men played female roles because women were unwilling to act. He made about 95 films and 26 shorts, including Lanka Dahan and Kaliya Mardan, but was ruined by the coming of sound. India's highest cinema honour, the Dadasaheb Phalke Award, is named after him.",
"Satyajit Ray":"Satyajit Ray (1921–1992), born in Kolkata to a family of writers, studied at Tagore's Santiniketan and worked as a graphic designer. Inspired by Italian neorealism (Bicycle Thieves), he made Pather Panchali (1955), about a poor Bengali boy, Apu, with non-professional actors and a tiny budget; it won a prize at Cannes. Ray went on to make Aparajito and Apur Sansar (the Apu Trilogy), Charulata, Jalsaghar and Shatranj Ke Khilari. He wrote the Feluda detective stories and Professor Shonku science fiction, and received an honorary Academy Award and the Bharat Ratna in 1992.",
"Lata Mangeshkar":"Lata Mangeshkar (1929–2022), daughter of the musician Deenanath Mangeshkar, began singing to support her family after her father's death when she was 13. She became the leading playback singer of Indian cinema from the late 1940s, recording thousands of songs in over 36 languages for films from Mahal (1949) to the 2000s. Her rendition of 'Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon' (1963), honouring soldiers who died in the war with China, reportedly moved Nehru to tears. She was awarded the Bharat Ratna in 2001 and called the 'Nightingale of India'.",
"Elvis Presley":"Elvis Presley (1935–1977) grew up poor in Tupelo, Mississippi and Memphis, Tennessee, absorbing gospel, country and Black rhythm and blues. His recordings at Sun Records from 1954 and hits like 'Heartbreak Hotel', 'Hound Dog' and 'Jailhouse Rock' made him the 'King of Rock and Roll'; his hip-shaking performances scandalised conservatives. His success also raised debates about white artists profiting from Black music. He served in the US Army in Germany, starred in 31 films, and died at 42.",
"Immanuel Kant":"Immanuel Kant (1724–1804) spent his entire life in Königsberg, Prussia, famous for his punctual daily walks. In the Critique of Pure Reason (1781) he argued that the mind actively structures experience through categories like space, time and causality, so we can know things only as they appear to us, not as they are in themselves. In ethics he proposed the categorical imperative: act only according to rules you could will to be universal laws, and treat people as ends, never merely as means. His essay 'What is Enlightenment?' (1784) urged 'Sapere aude — dare to know', and he proposed a federation of states for perpetual peace.",
"René Descartes":"René Descartes (1596–1650), a French philosopher and mathematician, served as a soldier during the Thirty Years' War and lived mostly in the Netherlands. In Discourse on the Method (1637) and Meditations, he doubted everything he could and found certainty in 'I think, therefore I am'. He argued mind and body are distinct substances (Cartesian dualism). He invented analytic geometry, the Cartesian coordinate system linking algebra and geometry. He died in Stockholm after being invited to tutor Queen Christina.",
"Karl Marx":"Karl Marx (1818–1883), born in Trier, studied philosophy and became a radical journalist; expelled from Germany, France and Belgium, he settled in London in 1849, supported by his friend Friedrich Engels. With Engels he wrote The Communist Manifesto (1848), declaring 'Workers of the world, unite!'. In Das Kapital (1867) he argued that capitalism exploits workers by extracting surplus value and would be replaced by socialism. He also wrote on British rule in India, criticising its brutality while seeing it as destroying old structures. His ideas inspired the Russian and Chinese revolutions and labour movements worldwide.",
"Friedrich Nietzsche":"Friedrich Nietzsche (1844–1900) became a professor of classics at Basel at 24 but retired due to illness. In works like Thus Spoke Zarathustra and Beyond Good and Evil he declared 'God is dead', criticised Christian morality as a 'slave morality', and imagined the Übermensch who creates his own values, and the 'eternal recurrence'. He suffered a mental collapse in 1889. His sister Elisabeth, a Nazi sympathiser, edited his writings to suit antisemitic nationalism, although Nietzsche had despised antisemitism.",
"Jean-Jacques Rousseau":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712–1778), born in Geneva, was a self-taught philosopher, composer and novelist. His Discourse on Inequality argued that private property and civilisation corrupted naturally good humans. The Social Contract (1762) opened 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains' and argued legitimate government rests on the people's 'general will'. Émile proposed natural education. His ideas inspired the French Revolution and Romanticism.",
"Voltaire":"François-Marie Arouet, 'Voltaire' (1694–1778), was a French writer imprisoned in the Bastille for satire and exiled to England, where he admired its freedoms. He championed religious tolerance and free speech, campaigning for victims of injustice like Jean Calas. His satirical novel Candide (1759) mocked naive optimism. He corresponded with Frederick the Great and Catherine the Great, and wrote Essay on the Customs and Spirit of Nations, which praised India and China.",
}
